import{_ as t,o as s,c as o,a2 as a}from"./chunks/framework.BUOFcz_J.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"The project Rho ships with","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"records/example-project-episode.md","filePath":"records/example-project-episode.md"}'),n={name:"records/example-project-episode.md"};function r(i,e,d,h,c,p){return s(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Historical record</p><p>This page records what happened on a date, with its observed failures and limits. It is not a requirement for the current implementation. Current behaviour is in <a href="/Rho/docs/reference/status">Status</a>.</p></div><h1 id="the-project-rho-ships-with" tabindex="-1">The project Rho ships with <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-project-rho-ships-with" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The project Rho ships with&quot;">​</a></h1><p>2026-11-01 local. Rho now starts with one finished research project, so a person who opens it sees what a project looks like when the work is done instead of an empty list. The study is the one in <a href="https://github.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Rho-cases</a>&#39;s <code>pa14-qs</code> case: <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em> PA14 quorum sensing and swarming, on that study&#39;s own data. Everything in the project was produced by an Agent in Rho on this machine; nothing was written to look finished.</p><p>The person who opens the copy decides what it is worth. What follows is what was built, what the real runs did, and what they exposed.</p><h2 id="what-ships" tabindex="-1">What ships <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-ships" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What ships&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>example/pa14-qs/</code> is a project folder with its own state, and <code>example/example.json</code> names the folder and the version it belongs to. A new service state copies it into the projects folder (<code>PA14 quorum sensing</code>) and registers it as an ordinary project whose record says it came from the example. A second start copies nothing; a folder the person already has is never overwritten; a person who deletes the copy does not get it back. <code>--example off</code> starts without it, and a service composed in code (<code>new RhoService({...})</code>) does not seed unless it asks to, so existing checks and acceptance scripts see the project lists they did before.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>In the project</th><th>What it holds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>README.md</code></td><td>The study, where the data come from, and what each file means</td></tr><tr><td><code>data/</code></td><td>The swarming assay, the co-culture assay, raw counts, sample sheet, annotation (1.2 MB)</td></tr><tr><td><code>analysis/</code></td><td>Four R scripts the Agent wrote: single mutants, co-culture, differential expression, summary figure</td></tr><tr><td><code>results/</code></td><td>Two figures, the summary figure, seven tables and <code>summary.md</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>project-notes.md</code></td><td>Three sections, one per session: question, data &amp; method, key results, choices with alternatives, files, to do</td></tr><tr><td><code>.rho/</code></td><td>The conversations, the versions each message kept, the commands, the Notebook cells and the timeline</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="the-runs" tabindex="-1">The runs <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-runs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The runs&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Claude Code (<code>westlakehpc/deepseek-v4.1-flash[1m]</code>, shown as DeepSeek V4.1 Flash) answered in Rho&#39;s own conversations, with Rho&#39;s grant and its native tools. Each question was asked in a person&#39;s words; the Agent was told nothing about how to answer it. The person&#39;s permission buttons were answered by the build harness: a command that stays inside the project is allowed, and anything reaching outside it, the network, credentials or a destructive command is refused. Nine requests were refused over the three sessions (a <code>find</code> outside the project, <code>sips</code>, a package check); the Studio shows them as failed commands.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Session</th><th>Question</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Do the las and rhl single mutants swarm worse than wild type, and does shape change?</td><td>Completed, 51 steps. Welch tests on log area with ratios of geometric means and Holm adjustment; all four mutants 0.13–0.29× wild type, circularity up 5.2–8.2×, roundness unchanged. Figure and two tables.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Do ΔrhlI + ΔlasI grown together swarm like wild type again, and does the ratio matter?</td><td>Completed, 64 steps. Fitted peak at 12.3:1 (95% CI 10.4–14.7), 1.34× wild type, ratio explained 93% of the variance in log area; the 500:1 mixes collapsed. Figure and two tables.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>What do the mutants change in gene expression?</td><td>Completed, 67 steps. DESeq2 over all 36 samples, nine mutant-vs-wild-type contrasts: 384–1,362 genes changed at FDR &lt; 0.05 and</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Hand on a table that is not 10 MB</td><td>Completed, 7 steps. The Agent wrote a compact table (7,143 rows, 15 columns) instead of 52,452 rows, and updated its notes.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Put the conclusion where it is easy to find</td><td>Completed, 19 steps. <code>results/summary.md</code>.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Draw the project&#39;s one-page summary figure</td><td>Completed, 24 steps. <code>results/summary.png</code>, which is what the Overview now leads with.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The scientific content is the case&#39;s study, so its reference answers can be read against it elsewhere; the case&#39;s questions and gold were not copied into the project, and the demo&#39;s questions are phrased in the person&#39;s own words.</p><h2 id="what-the-first-run-exposed" tabindex="-1">What the first run exposed <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-first-run-exposed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the first run exposed&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>A conversation could not be continued after Rho restarted.</strong> The first attempt to ask the second question in the session that answered the first failed with <code>Resource not found: &lt;session&gt;</code>: the Agent&#39;s session belongs to the process that ran it, and Rho retained only its identity. A probe of two services on one state directory reproduced it on an ordinary project with one short message — every conversation in a project became unreplyable after a quit-and-reopen, and a project copied to another machine could never reply at all.</p><p>Rho now reads that answer as what it is. When the Agent reports that the session it was asked to load is gone, the message runs in a new session, which is told what was said in the conversation so far — the person&#39;s words and the Agent&#39;s own replies, bounded to 12 KB and dropping the earliest exchanges first with a note that it did so. The turn records which session it replaced (<code>continued</code>), and the conversation says so in the person&#39;s own view: &quot;The Agent session this conversation had been using had ended, so this message ran in a new one, told what was said here before.&quot; Nothing is resent as a new request, and the messages Rho kept stay the record.</p><p>Observed on the shipped copy: a reply in the first session ran in a new session, answered &quot;which single mutant swarmed worst&quot; from the project&#39;s own files, and added the caveat that the two plates have their own wild-type control (geometric mean 108,686 here against 81,732 in the single-mutant plates).</p><p><strong>An Agent that iterates on a figure can outlast one message.</strong> The co-culture answer was stopped at the 30-minute wall limit of one message after 57 steps, with its figure already written. That attempt was removed and asked again with a raised <code>conversations.turnWallMs</code>; the demo must not ship a stopped message. The limit is right for a product and wrong for a build that asks for polished figures.</p><h2 id="what-the-copy-is-not" tabindex="-1">What the copy is not <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-copy-is-not" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the copy is not&quot;">​</a></h2><p>One recorded result names a file that is no longer there: the full differential-expression table was replaced by the compact one at the person&#39;s request, so a view that asks for the first table&#39;s bytes is answered &quot;This file is not in the project any more.&quot; That is the state a real project reaches when a derived file is superseded, and Rho says so.</p><p>The recorded folder in the commands and the Notebook is the folder this build ran in; on another machine that text is history, not a location. The shipped copy states the folder the build ran in and the host that ran it as the copy&#39;s own name, through the assembly step that also replaces the model catalogue with the one model the conversations used; the build folder keeps all three. The sessions the Notebook lists ended with the service that ran them, and Rho says so where it lists them. The copy is a project, not a tour: it does not explain Rho, and its notes are the Agent&#39;s scientific notes, not product documentation.</p><h2 id="checks" tabindex="-1">Checks <a class="header-anchor" href="#checks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Checks&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>node --test test/example-project.test.mjs</code> covers the copy (once, silently on a second start, never over a folder the person has) and the history a new session is given (what was said, newest kept, the budget, a first message carrying nothing). <code>npm run build</code> and <code>tsc -p tsconfig.web.json --noEmit</code> pass with the contract field and the view change.</p><p>The browser side was read at 1440 × 900 on the seeded copy: the projects list, the Overview leading with the summary figure, Studio with the notes and the run&#39;s commands, the Graph (six questions, 32 files, 50 runs, 111 versions kept, three conversations), and the Notebook with 42 recorded cells and an ended session. A reply in the seeded copy was answered as described above.</p>',22)])])}const w=t(n,[["render",r]]);export{u as __pageData,w as default};
